Priya: Hey team — noticed a deadlock/leak pattern in the Go generator sampleapp when a request deadline fires during streaming. The caller waits on it.Result() but the streaming goroutine never returns if the context was canceled mid-handshake. Repro below.

Joel: Can you paste the minimal repro?

Priya: Sure:
```go
func fetch(ctx context.Context, g *sdk.Generator, req sdk.Request) (string, error) {
  // caller sets a short ctx deadline
  iter, err := g.Generate(ctx, req)
  if err != nil { return "", err }
  // iterate tokens and build output
  for tok := range iter.Stream() {
    out += tok
  }
  // Result() blocks until stream is closed; if ctx was canceled during handshake, stream never closed
  return iter.Result(), nil
}
```

Lina: Ah, we've seen similar. The core issue is that the transport's handshake goroutine can be stuck waiting on auth refresh or TLS probe; the SDK's iterator relies on that goroutine to signal EOF. If the outer ctx is canceled, some internal channels are left unread.

Joel: Two quick suggestions: (1) add an explicit Close/Cancel method on the iterator to force cleanup; (2) make Result() non-blocking with a context parameter so callers can avoid indefinite waits.

Priya: For now I'm using a defensive pattern: spawn a goroutine to consume Stream() into a channel and select on ctx.Done() to stop early, then call iter.Abort() (we added a tiny helper in my fork). Pattern:
```go
resCh := make(chan string)
go func() {
  for t := range iter.Stream() { resCh <- t }
  close(resCh)
}()
select {
case <-ctx.Done():
  iter.Abort() // helper that closes internal transport and triggers goroutine cleanup
  return "", ctx.Err()
case out := <-resCh:
  return out, nil
}
```

Lina: We should standardize an Abort/Close on the iterator in the official SDK. Also document that reusing Generator across heavy cancel/retry workloads can lead to weird buffering.

OpsBot: Created issue #4988 "go-sdk: iterator deadlock when context cancelled mid-handshake" and linked a minimal repro gist.

Joel: I'll open a small PR to add Iter.Close(ctx) and make Result(ctx) accept a context. We'll keep the old API but mark deprecated. That buys compatibility and a safe upgrade path.

Priya: Thanks! I'll add the defensive sample to the quickstart and a note in the streaming FAQ about token refresh racing with handshake (causes intermittent hangs on mobile networks).

Lina: Also add a test that simulates auth refresh by forcing a mid-handshake 401 and ensure iter.Close() cleans up. Could catch regressions.

Priya: PR #221 opened with the sample + tests. CI flagged a flake on darwin-amd64, assigning to eng-infra to check timing.

OpsBot: PR linked: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk-go/pull/221 (ci/status: pending)

